#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068508 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d54bab77-d77f-5275-b454-ddb565eaa280" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indefinite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/id #uuid "f374b567-9a69-57ae-88eb-644ebfc451d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-506" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int  \\dfrac{5x}{x^2+3x-4} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\nx^2+3x-4 = (x-1)(x+4) \\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}+3x-4} & =\\frac{5x}{(x-1)(x+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{A}{(x-1)}+\\frac{B}{(x+4)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & =A(x+4)+B(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-4) & =A(-4+4)+B(-4-1) \\\\ -20 & =-5B \\\\ B & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, by plugging in $x=1,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(1) & =A(1+4)+B(1-1) \\\\ 5 & =5A \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5x}{x^2+3x-4}  = \\dfrac{1}{(x-1)} +\\dfrac{4}{(x+4)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}+3x-4}\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x-1}+\\frac{4}{x+4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\text{d}x+4\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068510 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "771dfcc1-f6c3-59ed-8961-3fe477b011b6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/id #uuid "fce14198-c8ee-50a6-981d-85df79cce761" :question/math-academy-id "e-507" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_1^4 \\dfrac{2x+3}{4x^2-1} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n4x^2-1 = (2x+1)(2x-1) \\,.\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{2x+3}{(2x+1)(2x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{A}{(2x+1)}+\\frac{B}{(2x-1)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =A(2x-1)+B(2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(\\frac{1}{2})+3 & =A(2(\\frac{1}{2})-1)+B(2(\\frac{1}{2})+1) \\\\ 4 & =2B \\\\ B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-\\frac{1}{2})+3 & =A(2(-\\frac{1}{2})-1)+B(2(-\\frac{1}{2})+1) \\\\ 2 & =-2A \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{4x^2-1} = -\\dfrac{1}{(2x+1)} +\\dfrac{2}{(2x-1)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{(2x+1)}+\\frac{2}{(2x-1)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{2x-1}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+2(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|)+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{2x-1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{|\\frac{2x-1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d54bab77-d77f-5275-b454-ddb565eaa280" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indefinite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "621e0753-637a-53cc-80ba-d6453bf77656" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316388} {:answer/id #uuid "20649eb5-4380-5341-a03b-575b1064c607" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316389} {:answer/id #uuid "eb3acafd-6d9e-51c7-b267-6ac02e6ffb8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316390} {:answer/id #uuid "b3db7dde-3eeb-57fb-bf71-8352b871b5d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316391} {:answer/id #uuid "2ebf77bd-d2d2-5584-9f55-2b454ea2c9b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "621e0753-637a-53cc-80ba-d6453bf77656" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316388} :answer-field/id #uuid "8acab6c7-5ae6-520f-a98a-219d03554f71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f96f4009-4009-5249-8f4f-45464ef56c36" :question/math-academy-id "q-103988" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2x+7}{(2x+1)(x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+7}{(2x+1)(x+2)} & =\\frac{A}{2x+1}+\\frac{B}{x+2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n2x+7=A(x+2)+B(2x+1).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-2)+7 & =A((-2)+2)+B(2(-2)+1) \\\\ 3 & =-3B \\\\ B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-\\frac{1}{2})+7 & =A(-\\frac{1}{2}+2)+B(2(-\\frac{1}{2})+1) \\\\ 6 & =A(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\ A & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+7}{(2x+1)(x+2)} & =\\frac{4}{2x+1}-\\frac{1}{x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+7}{(2x+1)(x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{4}{2x+1}-\\frac{1}{x+2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316393 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d54bab77-d77f-5275-b454-ddb565eaa280" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indefinite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "475c0859-7fcc-5659-abd8-58e1b393c37c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316395} {:answer/id #uuid "293dbcb2-7a2d-5e82-9de9-5dbace7662f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316396} {:answer/id #uuid "96b35e10-146b-5f3e-aff3-06c6f77981b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316397} {:answer/id #uuid "669d5ef9-aaf0-5f35-b7b5-80d20c0be133" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316398} {:answer/id #uuid "62e81304-d6ca-5bfb-88a4-14d4b95923a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96b35e10-146b-5f3e-aff3-06c6f77981b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316397} :answer-field/id #uuid "c88c9f99-3dd9-520b-bbe3-2b38faec9e4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "225ad7f2-9d35-5125-9718-e80b689027c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-103989" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2x-4}{x(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x-4}{x(x-4)} & =\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{x-4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n2x-4=A(x-4)+Bx.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(4)-4 & =A(4-4)+B(4) \\\\ 4 & =4B \\\\ 1 & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)-4 & =A(0-4)+B(0) \\\\ -4 & =-4A \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x-4}{x(x-4)} & =\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{x-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x-4}{x(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{x-4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316400 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d54bab77-d77f-5275-b454-ddb565eaa280" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indefinite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "063f1ba8-4c3c-561c-bac3-a1c9e574b445" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316402} {:answer/id #uuid "e07ea40e-336b-5666-9e7b-4e6ed410e0e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316403} {:answer/id #uuid "e10550fb-c460-5822-a39c-8c03f6983e88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316404} {:answer/id #uuid "5febc313-80ea-5212-ba9f-83b72a41adc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316405} {:answer/id #uuid "6f197432-474f-5427-9398-135f0bdd8ce0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e10550fb-c460-5822-a39c-8c03f6983e88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316404} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dd3ff56-e95b-552c-b17d-f6e1948d54c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d8d217e5-8474-5d6b-93f2-6f6e1253c6f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-103991" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{7}{(x-1)(x+6)}\\,\\text{d}x=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+b\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+6|+C,\n$$\n\nfind the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{(x-1)(x+6)} & =\\frac{A}{x-1}+\\frac{B}{x+6},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n7=A(x+6)+B(x-1).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-6,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7 & =A(-6+6)+B(-6-1) \\\\ 7 & =-7B \\\\ B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=1,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7 & =A(1+6)+B(1-1) \\\\ 7 & =7A \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{(x-1)(x+6)} & =\\frac{1}{x-1}-\\frac{1}{x+6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{7}{(x-1)(x+6)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x-1}-\\frac{1}{x+6})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{x+6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+6|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=1,b=-1,$ and $a+b=1-1=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316407 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "771dfcc1-f6c3-59ed-8961-3fe477b011b6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0821478-83eb-59d8-97d4-2ce35fb67f6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(\\frac{40}{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316409} {:answer/id #uuid "2e45f25c-a82e-56d5-86c7-88c374fe3ee1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0821478-83eb-59d8-97d4-2ce35fb67f6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(\\frac{40}{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316409} :answer-field/id #uuid "08f26187-1e8c-54aa-909c-f5528b7d14c1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "59ae9eeb-0243-5756-8fc5-d7e77b5d4115" :question/math-academy-id "q-347077" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{2x+2}{(x-2)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+2}{(x-2)(x+4)} & =\\frac{A}{(x-2)}+\\frac{B}{(x+4)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n2x+2=A(x+4)+B(x-2).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-4)+2 & =A(-4+4)+B(-4-2) \\\\ -6 & =-6B \\\\ B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2)+2 & =A(2+4)+B(2-2) \\\\ 6 & =6A \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x+2}{(x-2)(x+4)}=\\frac{1}{x-2}+\\frac{1}{x+4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+2}{(x-2)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x-2}+\\frac{1}{x+4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+2}{(x-2)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x-2)(x+4)|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{2x+2}{(x-2)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x-2)(x+4)|{|}_{3}^{6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(40)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\  & =ln\\,(\\frac{40}{7}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316411 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "771dfcc1-f6c3-59ed-8961-3fe477b011b6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df773fbb-4718-50c5-b714-941767347503" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{19}{2}\\ln(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df773fbb-4718-50c5-b714-941767347503" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{19}{2}\\ln(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316413} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d33bf1f-1818-5353-ae39-20a45ce557a9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "709449fa-0da7-5a66-8046-39e781e57c92" :question/math-academy-id "q-348975" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{3x+7}{{x}^{2}-8x+12}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that the denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-8x+12=(x-2)(x-6).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x+7}{{x}^{2}-8x+12} & =\\frac{3x+7}{(x-2)(x-6)}=\\frac{A}{x-2}+\\frac{B}{x-6},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n3x+7=A(x-6)+B(x-2).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=6,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(6)+7 & =A(6-6)+B(6-2) \\\\ 25 & =4B \\\\ B & =\\frac{25}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2)+7 & =A(2-6)+B(2-2) \\\\ 13 & =-4A \\\\ A & =-\\frac{13}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+7}{{x}^{2}-8x+12}=-\\frac{13}{4(x-2)}+\\frac{25}{4(x-6)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x+7}{{x}^{2}-8x+12}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{13}{4(x-2)}+\\frac{25}{4(x-6)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\frac{25}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-6|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x+7}{{x}^{2}-8x+12}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{13}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\frac{25}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-6|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{3x+7}{{x}^{2}-8x+12}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[-\\frac{13}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\frac{25}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-6|]}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{13}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+\\frac{25}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1))-(-\\frac{13}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+\\frac{25}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-\\frac{25}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{19}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316414 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "771dfcc1-f6c3-59ed-8961-3fe477b011b6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "725be603-15ff-5875-b99e-abd4b9f3e038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316416} {:answer/id #uuid "6d687db5-537f-5253-8bd2-1cb184f94320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(\\frac{121}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d687db5-537f-5253-8bd2-1cb184f94320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(\\frac{121}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316417} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b2b73e0-5c91-54e3-976b-a42f3d542b56" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "14cfb232-8881-5dc6-aba7-20fb95844fe5" :question/math-academy-id "q-349041" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}-19x-4=(5x+1)(x-4).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4} & =\\frac{15x-39}{(5x+1)(x-4)}=\\frac{A}{5x+1}+\\frac{B}{x-4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n15x-39=A(x-4)+B(5x+1).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15(4)-39 & =A(4-4)+B(5(4)+1) \\\\ 21 & =21B \\\\ B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{5},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15(-\\frac{1}{5})-39 & =A(-\\frac{1}{5}-4)+B(5(-\\frac{1}{5})+1) \\\\ -42 & =-\\frac{21}{5}A \\\\ A & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}=\\frac{10}{5x+1}+\\frac{1}{x-4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{10}{5x+1}+\\frac{1}{x-4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(5x+1)^{2}(x-4)|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(5x+1)^{2}(x-4)|{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(242)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{242}{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{121}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069300 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d54bab77-d77f-5275-b454-ddb565eaa280" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indefinite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2ab0f3a-4e41-524f-bd9e-03c46311722b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067332} {:answer/id #uuid "c255a472-9b1a-56de-baec-93fc501ee751" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067333} {:answer/id #uuid "c9f7b7a9-f5dc-5e8b-81ea-22c5213943d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067334} {:answer/id #uuid "17efe871-acac-5c1b-affc-45c1fa349a36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067335} {:answer/id #uuid "4b41ab18-e879-5684-9158-ed41d186f5c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c255a472-9b1a-56de-baec-93fc501ee751" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067333} :answer-field/id #uuid "c334a28a-25dc-5066-8861-a6d8339b2304" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1f6ebcba-4e39-5954-8ee8-c37c592d0f6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-49604" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{13}{(3x+2)(2x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-3|+b\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+C,\n$$\n\nfind the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13}{(3x+2)(2x-3)} & =\\frac{A}{3x+2}+\\frac{B}{2x-3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n13=A(2x-3)+B(3x+2).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13 & =A(2(\\frac{3}{2})-3)+B(3(\\frac{3}{2})+2) \\\\ 13 & =\\frac{13}{2}B \\\\ B & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{2}{3},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13 & =A(2(-\\frac{2}{3})-3)+B(3(-\\frac{2}{3})+2) \\\\ 13 & =-\\frac{13}{3}A \\\\ A & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13}{(3x+2)(2x-3)}=-\\frac{3}{3x+2}+\\frac{2}{2x-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{13}{(3x+2)(2x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{3}{3x+2}+\\frac{2}{2x-3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{3}{3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2}{2x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-3|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=1,b=-1,$ and $a+b=1-1=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069303 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "771dfcc1-f6c3-59ed-8961-3fe477b011b6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d056e470-bbb9-5efb-b3f7-6cdfec45ac75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{7}{32})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067350} {:answer/id #uuid "e55741ee-a7df-58bb-b3d1-29e8c87a8db6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (17)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067351} {:answer/id #uuid "0ace05db-2ed0-5e1d-af7d-be3349a9a280" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (168)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067352} {:answer/id #uuid "175944fd-56bb-51ab-86fe-5afb691675a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{7}{36})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067353} {:answer/id #uuid "6a00476a-749c-5bf2-8c69-b7b60c40a397" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{7}{24})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a00476a-749c-5bf2-8c69-b7b60c40a397" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{7}{24})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067354} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cba9533-abbc-5460-b1bf-3b10532ae935" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1b7a0d53-d8cd-5e6c-b6e9-1877172e8d87" :question/math-academy-id "q-49761" :question/problem "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{3x+4}{{x}^{2}-16}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-16=(x+4)(x-4).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x+4}{{x}^{2}-16} & =\\frac{3x+4}{(x+4)(x-4)}=\\frac{A}{(x+4)}+\\frac{B}{(x-4)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n3x+4=A(x-4)+B(x+4).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(4)+4 & =A(4-4)+B(4+4) \\\\ 16 & =8B \\\\ B & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-4,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-4)+4 & =A(-4-4)+B(-4+4) \\\\ -8 & =-8A \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+4}{{x}^{2}-16}=\\frac{1}{(x+4)}+\\frac{2}{(x-4)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x+4}{{x}^{2}-16}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{(x+4)}+\\frac{2}{(x-4)})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x+2{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x+4}{{x}^{2}-16}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x-4)^{2}|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+4)(x-4)^{2}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{3x+4}{{x}^{2}-16}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+4)(x-4)^{2}|{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(24) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{24}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316418 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d54bab77-d77f-5275-b454-ddb565eaa280" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indefinite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "106ec971-3076-5fe9-bb7d-7ccc214d1c16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316420} {:answer/id #uuid "fef1fe02-ebcd-56b4-9b67-52969f879015" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316421} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed0d6f6-0e1f-5f65-a227-9aa317a12416" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316422} {:answer/id #uuid "8a03c7b7-1ddb-5c8e-b1d8-7d0875030bc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316423} {:answer/id #uuid "482f3f93-5195-5956-8b99-24e240412db9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ed0d6f6-0e1f-5f65-a227-9aa317a12416" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316422} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d153439-f0e3-571f-8879-41cdbbccd805" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c65a481a-dbf5-5454-ad66-3a2df91cdbe5" :question/math-academy-id "q-49903" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{6x+12}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6x+12}{x(x+3)} & =\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{x+3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n6x+12=A(x+3)+Bx.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(-3)+12 & =A(-3+3)+B(-3) \\\\ -6 & =-3B \\\\ B & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(0)+12 & =A(0+3)+B(0) \\\\ 12 & =3A \\\\ A & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6x+12}{x(x+3)}=\\frac{4}{x}+\\frac{2}{x+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{6x+12}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{4}{x}+\\frac{2}{x+3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316425 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "771dfcc1-f6c3-59ed-8961-3fe477b011b6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29228550-4e97-5904-a7c7-467c5e70f2bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316427} {:answer/id #uuid "05c4b379-dcf0-59a4-8582-f3ec5b5191a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316428} {:answer/id #uuid "ef95d2f7-960e-56a1-acdd-2683ff67d708" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{8}{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316429} {:answer/id #uuid "34a25c5f-0dde-5ad6-bec6-f91e14f37efa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(28)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316430} {:answer/id #uuid "1291d401-1838-566b-a356-1b81bf63defc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1291d401-1838-566b-a356-1b81bf63defc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316431} :answer-field/id #uuid "38747f9e-0786-5d9e-90d4-91755e935282" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cf0cd4f5-d657-58c6-bc41-65da13b8c417" :question/math-academy-id "q-75828" :question/problem "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{4}^{11}\\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}+16x+3=(5x+1)(x+3).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3} & =\\frac{14}{(5x+1)(x+3)}=\\frac{A}{(5x+1)}+\\frac{B}{(x+3)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n14=A(x+3)+B(5x+1).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14 & =A(-3+3)+B(5(-3)+1) \\\\ 14 & =-14B \\\\ B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{5},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14 & =A(-\\frac{1}{5}+3)+B(5(-\\frac{1}{5})+1) \\\\ 14 & =\\frac{14}{5}A \\\\ A & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}=\\frac{5}{5x+1}-\\frac{1}{(x+3)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{5}{5x+1}-\\frac{1}{x+3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{5}{5x+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{5x+1}{x+3}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{11}\\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{5x+1}{x+3}|{|}_{4}^{11} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
